Inheritance is an OOP concept in Java, which allows the creation of a subclass (derived/child class) from a superclass (parent/base class). The subclass inherits all the properties and behaviors of the superclass, and can also have additional properties and behaviors of its own. Here's an example:

```java
// Defining a superclass Animal 
class Animal{
    String name;
    int age;

    // Constructor
    public Animal(String name, int age){
        this.name = name;
        this.age = age;
    }

    public void eat(){
        System.out.println(this.name + " is eating.");
    }

    public void makeNoise(){
        System.out.println(this.name + " is making some noise.");
    }
}
 //Defining a subclass Dog  
class Dog extends Animal{
    String breed;

    // Constructor
    public Dog(String name, int age, String breed){
        super(name, age); // calling the superclass constructor
        this.breed = breed;
    }

    public void wagTail(){
        System.out.println("The dog is wagging its tail.");
    }

    // overriding the makeNoise method
    @Override
    public void makeNoise(){
        System.out.println("The dog is barking.");
    }
}

// -------------------------------------------------------------------------
public class Main{
    public static void main(String[] args){
        // Creating a Dog object
        Dog dog1 = new Dog("Scooby", 5, "Great Dane");
        System.out.println(dog1.name + " is a " + dog1.breed + ", who is " + dog1.age + " years old.");
        dog1.eat();
        dog1.makeNoise();
        dog1.wagTail();
    }
} 
/*
Output:
Scooby is a Great Dane, who is 5 years old.
Scooby is eating.
The dog is barking.
The dog is wagging its tail.
*/
```

In this example, `Animal` is the superclass, and `Dog` is its subclass. `Dog` extends `Animal`, meaning it inherits all the properties and methods of the `Animal` class. In the `Dog` class, we have added an additional property `breed` and an additional method `wagTail()`. We have also overridden the `makeNoise` method to give it a different implementation.

