A linked list is a linear collection of data elements or nodes, where each node points to the next node in the list. Reversing a linked list means reversing the order of the nodes, where the first node becomes the last and the last node becomes the first. There are several ways to reverse a linked list in Java, including using an iterative approach or a recursive approach.

Here is an example of how to reverse a singly linked list using an iterative approach:

```java
public void reverseList(Node head) {
    Node prev = null; // Initialize a previous node pointer to null
    Node curr = head; // Start from the head of the list

    while (curr != null) {
        Node next = curr.next; // Save the next node in a temporary variable
        curr.next = prev; // Reverse the link, set the current node's next pointer to the previous node
        prev = curr; // Move the previous node pointer to the current node
        curr = next; // Move to the next node in the list
    }

    // Finally, update the head of the list to the new head
    head = prev;
}
```

This approach uses an iterative while loop to traverse the list and reverse the links between the nodes. We start from the head of the list and use a temporary variable to save the next node in the list. Then we set the current node's next pointer to point to the previous node instead of the next node. We do this by updating the previous node pointer to the current node and moving to the next node in the list until the entire list is reversed.

An alternate approach is to do this recursively:

```java
public Node reverseList(Node head) {
    // Base case: If the head is null or there is only one node in the list, return head
    if (head == null || head.next == null) {
        return head;
    }

    // Recursively reverse the rest of the list
    Node newHead = reverseList(head.next);

    // Reverse the current node
    head.next.next = head;
    head.next = null;

    // Return the new head of the reversed list
    return newHead;
}
```

